41367:$d$ is not divisible by $q[r]_q$. So, in order to determine $n_q(k,r;s)$ we can look at all $d_r$ that are multiples of $q[r]_q$. Equation~(\ref{eq_dr_C}) uniquely determines the auxiliary parameters $\sigma$, $\varepsilon_{r+1},\varepsilon_{r+2},\dots,\varepsilon_{k-1}$, which then determine the length $n$ and the minimum Hamming distance $d$ of the desired Griesmer code $C$. If $d_r$ is large, so is $d$, so that we only need to check the existence of the Griesmer codes for some small multiples of $q[r]_q$, since Griesmer codes always exist if $d$ is sufficiently large. For e.g.\ $q=3$, $k=5$, $r=2$, and $d_r=4\cdot 12=48$ we look for an $[55,5,36]_3$ Griesmer code, which indeed exists, so that $n_3(5,2;7)=55$. For the same parameters and $d_r=3\cdot 12=36$ we look for an $[41,5,25]_3$, which indeed exists. Since it is not a Griesmer code for $d_r=4\cdot 12=48$ we end up with $s=7$, the cases $s=6$ needs to be considered separately.
