The viable family is the paper’s exact “\(h\)-partitionability” witness problem, not “construct a Griesmer-optimal code.” Section 2 defines a multiset of projective points as \(h\)-partitionable when it is exactly the incidence sum of \(h\)-spaces; Section 4 shows that integer solvability can be characterized by Smith normal form but that the required nonnegative decomposition remains an ILP-style hard challenge. I’m specializing to binary lines: partition a planted set of nonzero bit-vectors into triples \(\{a,b,a\oplus b\}\). This keeps verification linear-time and avoids the paper’s explicit high-distance constructions, which would be an easy closed-form family.
