Implemented and verified the problem generator.

- [gen_2412_14615.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.14615/gen_2412_14615.py) — standard-library-only planted binary projective-line partitions.
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.14615/selftest_report.json) — all G1–G8 gates pass.
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.14615/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl) — script-generated oracle evidence.
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.14615/README.md) — definition, hardness boundary, worked example, results, usage, and caveats.

Key results:

- Shipping preset: `medium`, with 288 points and 96 required lines.
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000.
- Five adversarial attacks: each solved 0/8 instances.
- Canonical-key checks: 60/60 invariant; 20/20 unrelated instances distinct.
- Oracle verdict: `hardened`; all three scoreable shipping-level vendors failed. One Grok timeout was excluded and redrawn.
- A weaker 72-point prototype was detected as easy by local backtracking and removed before the final hardening run.

The family comes from the paper’s exact \(h\)-partitionability definition, specialized to binary projective lines; the paper’s explicit Griesmer constructions and other easy regimes are deliberately avoided.
